.Dd @MDOCDATE@
.Dt CLOWN 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm clown
.Nd coding-agent wrapper with prompt injection and plugin loading
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar query
.Nm
.Cm version
.Nm
.Fl -provider Ar name
.Op Ar args
.Nm
.Fl -naked
.Op Ar args
.Nm
.Cm restart
.Fl -session Ar key
.Op Fl -when Cm idle | now
.Op Fl -reason Ar text
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a Go binary that wraps
.Xr claude-code 1
with system prompt injection, managed security settings, plugin
lifecycle management, and parameterized plugin loading.
All unrecognized arguments are forwarded to the underlying provider CLI.
.Pp
By default,
.Nm
disables a configurable set of Claude built-in tools (see
.Sx DISALLOWED TOOLS
below) and auto-mode, injects hierarchical system prompts from
.Pa .clown/
directories, launches HTTP MCP servers declared in plugin
.Xr clown-json 5
manifests, and loads Claude Code plugins specified at build time.
.Pp
.Nm
directly manages the plugin server lifecycle that was previously
delegated to a separate
.Xr clown-plugin-host 1
process.
The standalone
.Xr clown-plugin-host 1
binary remains available for backward compatibility.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm version
Print version information for all components (clown, claude-code, codex,
and loaded plugins) and exit.
.It Cm restart Fl -session Ar key
Queue a restart of the live session
.Ar key
for its next idle transition, or with
.Fl -when Cm now
ask spinclass at once: spinclass's restart hand-off.
See
.Xr clown-restart 1 .
.It Fl -provider Ar name
Select the provider CLI.
Valid values are
.Cm claude
(default),
.Cm codex ,
.Cm juggler ,
.Cm opencode ,
.Cm openrouter ,
.Cm crush ,
and
.Cm clownbox
(disabled in the shipped build).
Can also be set via the
.Ev CLOWN_PROVIDER
environment variable.
.It Fl -profile Ar name
Apply the named profile: provider, backend, model, credentials, environment
and any saved MCP tool selection.
Also settable via
.Ev CLOWN_PROFILE .
Without either, a clownfile pin, the interactive picker or the build's
default profile chooses one.
See
.Xr clown-profiles 5 .
.It Fl -cheap-context
Pick which MCP servers and tool groups to keep before the provider starts,
and optionally save the choice into a profile.
Needs an interactive terminal; see
.Xr clown-profiles 5 .
.It Cm profile Ar verb
Manage the user profile registry:
.Cm list ,
.Cm add ,
.Cm edit Ar name
or
.Cm remove Ar name ;
see
.Xr clown-profiles 5 .
.It Fl -naked
Bypass all clown wrapping (prompt injection, plugin loading, tool
restrictions) and exec the provider CLI directly.
Not supported with
.Cm --provider opencode
or
.Cm crush ,
nor with
.Fl -tent ,
.Fl -agent-scope ,
.Fl -mcp-collapse
or
.Fl -print-launch-plan .
.It Fl -no-attach
Run inline, without the multiplexer wrap the
.Xr clownfile 5
.Sy [attach]
table configures, for this invocation only.
It wins over
.Sy [attach]
and
.Ev CLOWN_ATTACH_FORCE ,
which are ambient defaults.
Together with
.Fl -clown-attach Ns = Ns Cm spawn
(an orchestrator's detached-worker launch) it is refused with exit status 2
and nothing is started, since the two contradict each other.
A restart relaunch of a session launched with it keeps it, and
.Cm clown resume
accepts it too.
.It Fl -agent-scope Ar spec
Run the provider confined in a transient systemd user unit described by
the spinclass-written
.Ar spec
(FDR 0020); also settable via
.Ev CLOWN_AGENT_SCOPE .
The launch records the absolute, symlink-resolved
.Ar spec
path for its claude session (in
.Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/clown/session-names.jsonl ,
which the unit cannot reach); if the record cannot be written, the
confined launch is refused.
It also records a sha256 of the boundary the
.Ar spec
sets: its
.Sy worktree ,
.Sy operator_home ,
.Sy binds_rw ,
.Sy binds_ro ,
.Sy deny_paths
(except
.Pa wayland-*
entries),
.Sy egress_allow
(where an absent list, meaning clown's default, differs from an empty one)
and the
.Sy sockets
purposes, each list cleaned, sorted and de-duplicated.
What spinclass rewrites on every spawn is not part of it:
.Sy env
(the description and identity), socket paths,
.Sy unit_name_hint
and
.Sy result_path .
A boundary change between two spawns of one worktree, such as an edited
sweatfile egress-allow or a spawn without
.Ev SSH_AUTH_SOCK
(no piggy-agent socket), refuses the resume: a new boundary means a new
session.
Any later launch of that session, by
.Cm clown resume ,
a direct
.Fl -resume Ar id
(also spelled
.Fl r Ar id ,
.Fl r Ns Ar id ,
.Fl -resume= Ns Ar id ;
a session UUID matches in any letter case), a
.Fl -session-id Ar id ,
.Ev CLOWN_SESSION_ID ,
or a restart (which passes
.Fl -resume
with the session's key), runs confined under the same
.Ar spec
even without
.Fl -agent-scope ,
or is refused: when the
.Ar spec
is gone or no longer loads, when its boundary differs from the one its
last confined launch recorded, or differs again by the time the unit is
launched, when a different one is given (a session's spec cannot be
changed, in path or boundary; a new spec means a new session), when the
.Ar spec
file lies inside its own worktree or a
.Sy binds_rw
entry, symlinks resolved (the unit could rewrite it), and under
.Fl -naked
or
.Fl -tent .
A confined launch must know the claude session it runs under:
.Fl c ,
the bare
.Fl -resume
picker, and
.Fl p
without
.Fl -session-id
or
.Fl -resume Ar id
are refused with
.Fl -agent-scope .
Every
.Fl -resume
and
.Fl -session-id
value is checked; repeated values that disagree are refused.
While any session is recorded as confined, a
.Fl -resume
search term (neither a session UUID nor the launch's own session key) is
refused, and so is a short-option cluster carrying
.Fl r
after its first letter (such as
.Fl cr ) ,
whose value cannot be read with certainty.
The records are never pruned, so once any session has run confined this
refusal holds for every later launch that uses the same
.Ev XDG_STATE_HOME ;
resume by UUID or with
.Cm clown resume
instead.
.Fl -fork-session
and
.Fl -from-pr
are refused for a confined session and under
.Fl -agent-scope :
the new session would carry no record.
A session never recorded as confined runs under the
.Ar spec
given, if any; a resume of one says it runs UNCONFINED.
.Pp
Not covered: a session started by
.Cm clown agent-scope-run
(a raw runner that neither checks nor records) or confined by a clown
older than this record; an unconfined
.Fl c
or bare
.Fl -resume
picker launch, where claude picks a conversation clown cannot check and
may continue a confined one (known gap, clown#326); and any launch run with a
different
.Ev XDG_STATE_HOME ,
which hides every record.
.It Fl -skip-failed
When any discovered HTTP MCP plugin server fails to start,
.Nm
proceeds with only the healthy subset instead of aborting.
Also settable via the
.Ev CLOWN_SKIP_FAILED_PLUGINS
environment variable.
.It Fl -disable-clown-protocol
Bypass the plugin server pipeline entirely: no
.Pa clown.json
discovery, no HTTP MCP server launch, and no plugin manifest
compilation.
Plugin directories are passed to Claude Code unmodified so claude's
native plugin loader (and its native MCP registration) is the only path
active.
Intended as an escape hatch for debugging or when native claude behavior
is preferred.
Also settable via the
.Ev CLOWN_DISABLE_CLOWN_PROTOCOL
environment variable.
.It Fl -print-launch-plan
Print the fully-resolved provider invocation to standard output as a
single line of JSON and exit 0 without spawning the provider.
The object has the keys
.Li binary ,
.Li args ,
.Li env ,
and
.Li files ;
.Li args
preserves argv order, while
.Li env
and
.Li files
are sorted.
Values of
.Li env
entries whose key matches
.Li TOKEN ,
.Li KEY ,
.Li SECRET ,
or
.Li PASSWORD
.Pq case-insensitive
are replaced with
.Li "<redacted>" .
.Li files
is currently always empty.
.Pp
Redaction is by environment-variable NAME only.
.Li args
is reproduced verbatim, including everything after
.Cm -- ,
so a credential passed on the command line
.Pq for example Cm -- --api-key Ar secret
appears in the plan unchanged.
Review a plan before pasting it into a bug report or committing it as a
test fixture.
.Pp
Everything upstream of the spawn still runs, including plugin
discovery and plugin server startup, because the plan's
.Li args
and
.Li env
are derived from it.
With
.Fl -tent
this also includes loading the container image, so the run is not
side-effect-free there.
The clownfile
.Li [attach]
multiplexer wrap is skipped, so the plan always reaches this process's
standard output rather than a multiplexer's screen.
.Pp
Not supported with
.Fl -naked
or
.Cm --provider codex ,
which exec-replace
.Nm
rather than spawning a child;
.Nm
exits non-zero rather than silently launching the provider.
.It Fl v , Fl -verbose
Emit informational progress on stderr in addition to the always-on
log file.
Without this flag, only errors, failure summaries, and the interactive
plugin-failure prompt are written to stderr; plugin servers' own
stderr output is captured to the log but not mirrored to the terminal.
.El
.Sh SYSTEM PROMPT INJECTION
.Nm
walks from
.Ev PWD
up to
.Ev HOME ,
collecting
.Pa .clown/
directories at each level.
.Ss Replace mode
If any directory contains
.Pa .clown/system-prompt ,
the deepest one replaces Claude's system prompt entirely via
.Fl -system-prompt-file .
.Ss Append mode
All
.Pa *.md
files found in
.Pa .clown/system-prompt.d/
directories are concatenated shallowest-first and appended to the
system prompt via
.Fl -append-system-prompt-file .
Builtin fragments shipped with
.Nm
are prepended before user fragments.
Plugins MAY contribute fragments by shipping
.Pa .clown-plugin/system-prompt-append.d/*.md
files inside their plugin directory; those fragments are layered
between the builtin and user fragments in plugin-list order, as
the consumer wrote them in
.Cm mkJuggler .
Append-mode fragments still flow through even when replace mode is
active: replace applies to the user-supplied prompt, not to
plugin-shipped instructions.
.Sh PLUGINS
.Nm
ships with zero default plugins.
Consumers supply plugin flake inputs via
.Fn mkJuggler
at build time (see
.Sx NIX INTERFACE
below).
Each plugin directory is passed to
.Xr claude-code 1
via
.Fl -plugin-dir .
.Pp
A plugin directory must contain
.Pa .claude-plugin/plugin.json
with at minimum a
.Fa name
field.
See
.Xr claude-code-settings 5
for the full plugin manifest schema.
.Ss HTTP MCP Servers
Plugins may also declare HTTP-based MCP servers in a
.Pa clown.json
manifest (see
.Xr clown-json 5 ) .
These servers are automatically launched and managed by
.Nm
directly: it scans plugin directories for
.Pa clown.json ,
launches declared servers, reads their handshake, polls health endpoints,
compiles replacement plugin manifests with URL-based MCP entries, and
passes the compiled directories to the provider CLI.
HTTP transport enables features unavailable over stdio, including
.Li notifications/tools/list_changed
and server-initiated requests.
.Sh NIX INTERFACE
.Nm
exports
.Fn lib.${system}.mkJuggler
within its flake outputs.
This function accepts a single attribute set:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mkJuggler {
  plugins = [
    { flake = moxy; dirs = [ "share/purse-first/moxy" ]; }
    { flake = bob;  dirs = [ "share/purse-first/*" ]; }
  ];
}
.Ed
.Pp
Each element in
.Fa plugins
is an attribute set with two fields:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fa flake
The plugin's flake input value.
Used to resolve the default package and extract revision metadata.
.It Fa dirs
A list of paths relative to the flake's default package output.
Each entry is either a literal path to a plugin directory or a glob
pattern.
Glob characters
.Pq Li * , Li \&? , Li \&[
trigger shell expansion at build time; non-plugin directories in
the expansion are silently skipped.
A glob that matches zero valid plugins fails the build.
.El
.Pp
The function returns an attribute set containing:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fa packages.default
The
.Nm
wrapper with plugins burned in.
.It Fa devShells.default
Development shell.
.It Fa checks
Build-time validation tests.
.El
.Ss Consumer example
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
  inputs = {
    clown.url = "github:amarbel-llc/clown";
    moxy.url = "github:amarbel-llc/moxy";
    bob.url = "github:amarbel-llc/bob";
  };

  outputs = { clown, moxy, bob, ... }:
    let
      system = "aarch64-darwin";
      juggler = clown.lib.${system}.mkJuggler {
        plugins = [
          { flake = moxy; dirs = [ "share/purse-first/moxy" ]; }
          { flake = bob;  dirs = [ "share/purse-first/*" ]; }
        ];
      };
    in {
      packages.${system}.default = juggler.packages.default;
    };
}
.Ed
.Sh DISALLOWED TOOLS
.Nm
passes
.Fl -disallowed-tools
flags to the provider CLI, preventing use of a build-time configurable
set of Claude built-in tools.
The list is read from a file baked into the Nix derivation.
The default set disables tools that are redundant with MCP-based
replacements provided by plugins (e.g.\&
.Nm moxy ) :
.Pp
.Bl -column "EnterWorktree" "Replacement" -compact
.It Sy Tool Ta Sy Replacement
.It Li Bash(*) Ta moxy tools
.It Li Glob Ta folio.glob
.It Li Grep Ta rg.search
.It Li LSP Ta \(em
.It Li Write Ta folio.write
.It Li WebFetch Ta chrest.web-fetch
.It Li WebSearch Ta \(em
.It Li EnterWorktree Ta spinclass
.It Li NotebookEdit Ta \(em
.It Li PowerShell Ta \(em
.El
.Pp
.Li Agent(Explore)
is not disallowed: the clown-hook-allow PreToolUse hook redirects an
Explore subagent to clown's read-only Discover subagent instead.
.Pp
To bypass all disallowed tools (and all other clown wrapping), use
.Fl -naked .
.Sh SETTINGS
.Nm
ships no managed settings and runs an unpatched
.Xr claude-code 1
(clown#133).
For the claude provider it passes a
.Fl -settings
document that sets, each only when the user has not:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Ev CLAUDE_AFK_TIMEOUT_MS
to its maximum, so an AskUserQuestion decision is never auto-continued;
.It
.Ev CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AUTO_MEMORY Ns = Ns Li 1 ;
.It
.Cm remoteControlAtStartup : Cm false
(always set);
.It
.Ev CLOWN_SESSION_ID ,
so hooks see the session identity;
.It
.Cm permissions.defaultMode : Cm dontAsk ,
only under
.Ev CLOWN_UNATTENDED Ns = Ns Li 1
(see
.Sx ENVIRONMENT ) .
.El
.Pp
Auto-mode and bypass-permissions mode are
.Em not
blocked (clown#143).
.Fl -settings
outranks user, project and local settings; a managed source on the host
can still override it.
See
.Xr claude-code-settings 5
for the precedence chain.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev CLOWN_PROVIDER
Default provider CLI.
Overridden by
.Fl -provider .
Valid values: as for
.Fl -provider .
.It Ev CLOWN_UNATTENDED
When set to exactly
.Li 1 ,
the launch runs claude in its
.Cm dontAsk
permission mode: a tool call that is not allowed is denied with no dialog,
and the session carries on (clown#323).
.Nm
adds
.Cm permissions.defaultMode : Cm dontAsk
to its
.Fl -settings
document; nothing is recorded, so every launch, restart and
.Cm clown resume
reads its own environment.
Unset or empty: claude's default mode.
Any other value is refused, and so is the marker with a provider other than
claude, with
.Fl -naked ,
or with provider args that pass another
.Fl -permission-mode ,
.Fl -dangerously-skip-permissions ,
.Fl -settings
or
.Fl -setting-sources .
Under
.Cm dontAsk
claude fires no PermissionRequest hook, so the
.Li clown.permission.*
counters count nothing.
.It Ev CLOWN_SKIP_FAILED_PLUGINS
When set to
.Li 1 ,
behaves the same as passing
.Fl -skip-failed .
.It Ev CLOWN_DISABLE_CLOWN_PROTOCOL
When set to
.Li 1 ,
behaves the same as passing
.Fl -disable-clown-protocol .
.It Ev CLOWN_STAGING_ROOT
Rollback lever for where the per-launch staging root is created.
Every artifact
.Nm
generates for a provider lives under one directory per launch, removed on
exit: compiled plugin dirs, the synthesized built-in plugins, the generated
.Xr opencode 1
and
.Xr crush 1
config, and the prompt-append file.
Its base is normally chosen per provider:
.Cm clownbox
gets
.Pa <cwd>/.tmp ,
so the artifacts fall inside the sandbox's repo bind-mount, and every other
provider gets
.Ev TMPDIR .
The only accepted value is
.Li tmpdir ,
which forces the base to
.Ev TMPDIR
regardless of provider.
An unrecognised value is reported on standard error and otherwise ignored,
leaving placement unchanged.
.Pp
This variable is
.Em temporary .
It exists so an artifact-placement regression can be ruled out in the field
without a revert, and will be removed after one release with no
artifact-placement reports across
.Cm claude ,
.Cm opencode
and
.Cm crush .
It cannot restore the pre-staging behaviour of scattering artifacts across
independent temporary directories; that is no longer representable.
.It Ev MCP_CONNECTION_NONBLOCKING
Set by
.Nm
to
.Li false ,
only if unset, in its own environment before it launches
.Cm claude
(not
.Fl -naked ,
not other providers), so
.Xr claude-code 1
connects its MCP servers before the first model request instead of sending
that request with only its built-in tools (clown#296).
Claude bounds the wait with its own
.Ev MCP_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS .
Set it yourself, e.g. to
.Li true ,
to keep claude's non-blocking connect.
.It Ev CLAUDE_CODE_MCP_STARTUP_WAIT_MS
Set by
.Nm
to
.Li 10000
for headless
.Cm claude
launches only, if unset: a detached spawn
.Pq Fl -clown-attach=spawn ,
a
.Fl p Ns / Ns Fl -print
run, or a launch whose stdin or stderr is not a terminal.
For a spawn wrapped in the
.Sy [attach]
multiplexer, the outer
.Nm
sets it, and it reaches claude only if the multiplexer passes the outer
environment to the inner
.Nm ;
the inner one runs on a real pty and does not add it itself.
.Pp
Neither variable is delivered to a
.Fl -tent
container, which receives only its fixed passthrough list.
An
.Fl -agent-scope
unit receives both.
Both variable names come from the claude binary rather than its
documentation and may change between claude versions.
.It Ev CLOWN_PLUGIN_META
Path to the plugin metadata directory containing
.Pa plugin-dirs
and
.Pa version-info
files.
Set automatically by the Nix wrapper; not intended for manual use.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa .clown/system-prompt
Replace-mode system prompt file (deepest wins).
.It Pa .clown/system-prompt.d/*.md
Append-mode prompt fragments (shallowest first).
.It Pa <plugin>/.clown-plugin/system-prompt-append.d/*.md
Plugin-shipped append-mode fragments.
Picked up at
.Cm mkJuggler
build time and layered between the builtin fragments and the user's
.Pa .clown/system-prompt.d/ .
.El
